## Overclaim register

1. Quote: "ALL have defect ≥ 0 (no monotonicity violation)" over 210 rows. Correction: true in the table [EXACT COMPUTATION]; a finite table is NEVER a uniform theorem — it does not imply `M≥L` uniformly or Collatz.
2. Quote: "the positive-lift set is EXACTLY {(4,2),(8,2),(10,2),(10,9),(11,9)}". Correction: exact within the 210-row table only; `(10,9),(11,9)` themselves were post-hoc additions beyond the prescribed grid, proving the set grows with the grid. A finite table is NEVER a uniform theorem.
3. Quote: "no row with m≥12 has L>0". Correction: table-scoped only; same history warns against promoting absence to a lemma (cf. dead #6 `s≥3` claim). A finite table is NEVER a uniform theorem.
4. Quote: "min M/L … is exactly 17/8". Correction: table minimum, not a uniform lower bound `M≥(17/8)L`. A finite table is NEVER a uniform theorem.
5. Quote: "radius-2 charging has zero violations over all intervals on the 210-row grid". Correction: `0/29835` confirmed, but `47.05%` of those intervals (41.99% outside `s=1`) have demand zero and pass vacuously; binding fraction is `≈0.5295` overall, `≈0.5801` non-`s=1`. The 23 `s=1` rows (2599 intervals) are degenerate (`L_k≡0`) and carry no charging information yet inflate the denominator.
6. Quote (H2): "pointwise-k charging is false — 8 witnesses, e.g. (18,2) k=14: lift 3/35 exceeds adjacent merge 4033/69615". Correction: 8 is the radius-1 singleton count; strict pointwise `L_k>M_k` has 46 failures on this grid. The quoted supply `4033/69615=M_14+M_15` is the adjacent sum, not `M_14=7741/139230`. Either rename the claim to radius-1-singleton or correct the count/supply.
7. Quote (H4): "EXACT global minima … 32/5, 48/5, 43/15, 14/5 — no violation anywhere". Correction: first three re-verified exhaustively here; `14/5` at `(6,3)` (6.1M families) was sampled only (30k trials, best `118/15`, no violation) and no producer argmin is on file, so that one minimum remains single-implementation. A finite table is NEVER a uniform theorem.
8. Quote (H6): per-frequency defects "strictly positive for BOTH odd frequencies". Correction: narrow conjecture, honestly scoped, but currently single-implementation with no decomposition spec in evidence; cite as [CONJECTURE]/[DIAGNOSTIC], not as verified fact.
9. Quote (H7): "for s=1 both sides vanish identically". Correction: only the lift side vanishes (`L≡0`, every `L_k=0`, verified on all 23 rows). `M>0` generally (e.g. `(3,1): M=4/3`) and `F≠Out`, so "both sides" is false. `s=1` rows carry zero information about lift/charging but still exercise masses/merge machinery.
10. Quote pattern: "verified 0 violations over 210 rows" / "independent" second implementations. Correction: any check sharing the producer's `H`, odd-starts-only, `J` normalisation, or transfer convention is not fully independent (see Common-mode). The `s=1` block (23/210 rows) must be excluded from any "evidence for the source" count.

## Common-mode error analysis

Risk: all three Round-8 sessions plausibly inherit the same conventions from the producer codebase: shortcut `H`, odd-starts-only histograms, mass `binom(m-1,k-1)`, energy normalisation `J_r=2^{r-1}Σdiff²`, transfer pair `(E_c,O_c)` with `r=3^{-1} mod N`, and the window conventions `[a,b+1]`/`[a-1,b+2]`. A shared mis-encoding (e.g. wrong `r`, wrong `c_k=3^k`, even starts mishandled, `J` off by a factor, folding mod the wrong modulus) would pass every "second implementation" that re-imports or retypes the same formulas.

What this audit did to rule part of it out [EXACT COMPUTATION]: direct lifted-pair counting (`y=x+3^k`, parity-split, `H` applied mod `q`) without calling any transfer helper, then asserted transfer-formula equality and `P'=E+O` merge equality against separately enumerated `P_{m+1}` on 198/210 rows — all passed. Count-sum `6446361` and generic minimum `-4/3` were reproduced from independent brute force. That rules out a transfer-coding common mode for the tested grid.

What remains (independent evidence still needed): (a) that odd-starts-only plus `binom` masses is the right model of the research question (even starts are excluded by definition — a convention error here is shared by design); (b) that `J`'s normalisation is the Fourier odd-character energy (my [NUM] check `J=N·Σ_odd|Phat|²` supports it up to the scale factor, but an exact Fourier-side proof was not re-done here); (c) the radius window off-by-one convention (`[a,b+1]` vs `[a-1,b+1]`) — my numbers confirm the prompt's convention reproduces the quoted violation `1934/69615`, but an off-by-one shared by all sessions would move violations between "pointwise / radius-1 / radius-2" labels (as seen in H2); (d) H6's entire decomposition and H4's `(6,3)` DP minimum remain single-implementation. A genuinely independent check would re-derive `J` from the Fourier definition, re-derive `(E_c,O_c)` from the parity-word affine form `x→(3^kx+B_w)/2^m` with odd `B_w`, and publish H6's per-`a` formulas plus the `(6,3)` argmin family.

